Hi,

Here are some (untested) suggestions.

It might be a class loader issue, what happens with these calls :

 > .jcall( "java.lang.Class", "Ljava/lang/Class;", "forName", 
"rif/RIFFrame" )

 > .jcall( "java/lang/Class", "Ljava/lang/Class;", "forName", 
"rif/RIFFrame", TRUE, .jcast( rJava:::.rJava.class.loader, 
"java/lang/ClassLoader" ) )


You could pass a String array of length 0, instead of null :
.jcall("rif/RIFFrame","V",method="main",.jarray(character(0)) )


I don't think calling library( JGR ) is the usual way to start JGR, a 
lot happens behind the scenes with the startup script, plus you would 
usually declare your package dependent on JGR rather than manually load 
JGR as part of your .First.lib.


Do you really need to load the JGR R package, or do you just need its jars ?

Romain


